270→## Wave Deformation
271→
272→### Animated Ramp-Driven Wave Patterns [[Ep2, 56:56](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3416s)]
273→```vex
274→float d = length(@P);
275→d *= ch('scale');
276→d -= @Time;
277→@P.y = chramp('my-map', d);
278→@P.y *= ch('height');
279→```
280→Creates animated wave patterns by calculating distance from origin, scaling and offsetting by time, then using a ramp parameter to control vertical displacement. The ramp lookup value is animated by subtracting @Time from the scaled distance, creating waves that propagate over time. The final height is multiplied by a height parameter for amplitude control.
281→
282→### Animated Waves with Ramp Control [[Ep2, 57:30](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3450s)]
283→```vex
284→float d = length(@P);
285→d *= ch("scale");
286→d -= @Time;
287→d %= 1;
288→d = sin(d);
289→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
290→@P.y = chramp("myRamp", d);
291→@P.y *= ch("height");
292→```
293→Creates animated wave deformations by calculating distance from origin, animating it with time, applying sine wave oscillation, and then using a ramp parameter to shape the wave profile. The chramp lookup allows artistic control over the wave shape by mapping the oscillating value through a customizable ramp curve, which is then scaled by a height multiplier.
294→
295→### Ramp and Distance Deformation [[Ep2, 58:14](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3494s)]
296→```vex
297→float d = length(@P);
298→d *= ch("scale");
299→d = abs(d);
300→d = sin(d);
301→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
302→@P.y = chramp("myram", d);
303→@P.y *= ch("height");
304→```
305→Creates a vertical deformation by calculating distance from origin, applying sine wave modulation, and using chramp() to lookup values from a ramp parameter. The distance is scaled and fitted to 0-1 range before being used as the ramp lookup value, which controls the Y position of points multiplied by a height parameter.
306→
307→### Distance-based Height with ID Offset [Needs Review] [[Ep2, 59:00](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3540s)]
308→```vex
309→float d = length(@P);
310→d *= ch('scale');
311→d += @id;
312→d += sin(d);
313→d *= ch('height');
314→@P.y = d;
315→```
316→Calculates a distance value from the origin using point position length, scales it with a channel parameter, offsets by point ID, applies a sine wave modulation, and uses the result to drive the Y position of points. The final line was corrected from 'ch('height')' to 'd' as the intent is to assign the calculated distance value to the point's Y coordinate.
317→
318→### Fit and Ramp for Wave Displacement [[Ep2, 59:26](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3566)]
319→```vex
320→float d = length(@P);
321→d *= ch("scale");
322→d -= sin(d);
323→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
324→@P.y = chramp("my-ramp", d);
325→@P.y *= ch("height");
326→```
327→This code creates a radial wave displacement by calculating distance from origin, applying sine wave modulation, and remapping the result through a ramp parameter for artistic control. The fit() function normalizes the sine wave's range from [-1,1] to [0,1] so it can properly drive the chramp() lookup, which then sets vertical position scaled by a height parameter.
328→
329→### Ramp-driven displacement with fit [[Ep2, 59:44](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3584s)]
330→```vex
331→float d = length(@P);
332→d *= ch("scale");
333→d += 0;
334→d *= sin(d);
335→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
336→@P.y = chramp("my-ramp", d);
337→@P.y *= ch("height");
338→```
339→Creates a radial displacement pattern by calculating distance from origin, applying sine wave modulation, fitting the result to 0-1 range, and using it to sample a ramp parameter for vertical displacement. The fit function clamps the sine wave oscillation to a normalized range suitable for ramp lookup, while position values themselves can extend beyond typical bounds.
340→
341→### Ramp-driven height displacement [[Ep2, 62:54](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3774s)]
342→```vex
343→float d = length(@P);
344→d *= ch("scale");
345→d *= @Time;
346→d = sin(d);
347→
348→@P.y = chramp("my-ramp", d);
349→@P.y *= ch("height");  // multiply d by @Time before sin() for time-varying oscillation
350→```
351→
352→### Ramp-Driven Height Displacement [Needs Review] [[Ep2, 68:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4092s)]
353→```vex
354→float d = length(@P);
355→d *= ch("scale");
356→d = sin(d);
357→d = sin(d);
358→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
359→@P.y = chramp("my-ramp", d) * ch("height");  // double sin() creates interference; fit() normalizes for chramp
360→```
361→
362→### Distance-Based Deformation with Ramps [[Ep2, 70:18](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4218s)]
363→```vex
364→float d = length(@P);
365→d *= ch('scale');
366→d *= sin(d);
367→d *= fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
368→d = chramp('my-ramp', d);
369→@P.y *= ch('height');
370→```
371→This code calculates distance from origin using length(@P), applies sinusoidal waves, normalizes the result with fit(), and uses a color ramp to control vertical displacement. The technique chains multiple operations to create controllable distance-based deformations with UI parameters for scale and height.
372→
373→### Animated Radial Wave Pattern [[Ep2, 71:02](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4262s)]
374→```vex
375→float d = length(@P);
376→d *= ch('scale');
377→d -= @Time;
378→d %= 1;
379→@P.y = chramp('my-ramp', d);
380→@P.y *= ch('height');  // d %= 1 creates repeating concentric rings for chramp lookup
381→```
382→
383→### Animated Radial Sine Wave [[Ep2, 71:14](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4274s)]
384→```vex
385→float d = length(@P);
386→d *= ch('scale');
387→d -= @Time;
388→d = sin(d);
389→d = fit(d, -1, 0, 0, 1);
390→d = chramp('my_control_ramp', d);
391→@P.y += ch('height');  // fit(d,-1,0,0,1) maps only negative half of sine to [0,1]
392→```
393→
394→### Distance-based height displacement with ramps [[Ep2, 74:32](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4472s)]
395→```vex
396→float d = length(@P);
397→v@Cd = ch('color');
398→d = sin(d);
399→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
400→d = chramp('height', d);
401→@P.y *= ch('height');
402→```
403→This example calculates the distance from the origin, applies a sine wave, normalizes it to 0-1 range, then uses a ramp parameter to control a vertical displacement multiplier. The code demonstrates core VEX patterns commonly used across production work, including distance fields, trigonometric functions, value remapping, and channel/ramp parameter integration.
404→
405→## Ramp-Driven Deformation
406→
407→### Ramp-Driven Height Displacement [[Ep2, 99:42](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=5982s)]
408→```vex
409→float d = length(@P);
410→d *= ch('pre_scale');
411→d = chramp('my_stepped_ramp', d);
412→d *= ch('post_scale');
413→@P.y = d;
414→```
415→This code calculates the distance from the origin using length(@P), scales it with a pre-multiplier channel, remaps the value through a stepped ramp parameter, applies a post-scale multiplier, and assigns the result to the Y position to create height displacement. The technique demonstrates how to chain multiple transformations including ramp lookups to achieve controlled deformation based on radial distance.
416→
417→## Wave Deformation
418→
419→### Point Displacement with Channels [[Ep2, 9:58](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=598s)]
420→```vex
421→float d = length(@P);
422→d *= ch('v_scale');
423→d = @Time;
424→@P.y = sin(d);
425→
426→@P += @N;
427→
428→@P += @N * ch('push');
429→```
430→Demonstrates multiple techniques for displacing points including calculating distance from origin, using time-based sine wave deformation, and offsetting geometry along normals with channel-controlled strength. The code shows progressive iterations of displacement methods, with the final version using a 'push' parameter to control normal-based offset magnitude.
431→
432→### Distance-Based Displacement [[Ep3, 10:42](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fOasE4T9BRY&t=642s)]
433→```vex
434→float d = length(@P);
435→d *= ch('scale');
436→@Cd = d;
437→@P.y = sin(d);
438→```
439→Calculates the distance of each point from the origin using length(@P), scales it with a channel parameter, assigns the distance to color, and displaces points vertically using a sine wave based on the scaled distance. The code demonstrates why @P can be written in point context but not in primitive context, as point positions are point-level attributes.
440→
441→### Distance to Surface Point [Needs Review] [[Ep3, 27:48](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fOasE4T9BRY&t=1668s)]
442→```vex
443→vector pos = point(1, @P);
444→float d = distance(@P, pos);
445→d *= chf("M1");
446→@Cd.r = sin(d);
447→```
448→Uses the point() function to find the closest point on a reference surface geometry (input 1) to the current point position, then calculates the distance between them. The distance is multiplied by a channel parameter and used with sine to create a color pattern in the red channel, producing proximity-based visualization.
